module bindings.ruby.rack_attack_throttle_raw_path_equality;

// A rack-attack throttle counts requests whose block returns a non-nil key:
//
//   throttle('login/ip', limit: 5, period: 5.minutes) do |req|
//     req.ip if req.path == '/auth/sign_in' && req.post?
//   end
//
// Rails routes the same action under any format suffix (`/auth/sign_in.json`),
// and Rack::Request#path returns the suffixed spelling, so a key predicate of
// raw path equality never holds for the suffixed request, the block returns
// nil, and the throttle counts nothing -- the brute-force limit that the
// configuration declares is not the one that runs. The remediation compares a
// derived accessor instead (any normalization of the path), and the two forms
// separate structurally on the binary token: `req.path == x` emits a token
// beginning `req.path==`, while every derived form (`req.path_without_extentions ==`,
// `req.path.sub(...) ==`, `req.path.start_with?(...)`) emits one that does not,
// because the character after the accessor name is not `=`. The startsWith
// boundary is what makes that distinction -- a contains needle of `req.path`
// matches both forms, and `call_arg_at` pairing does not exist in Ruby.
//
// The two conjuncts co-occur at module scope, so they combine by co-occurrence
// in the initializer file: a file that throttles one endpoint and compares a
// raw request path for equality anywhere else reports as well. Across every
// Ruby file in the corpus clones (12007 files; a throttle call occurs in three
// projects' rack-attack initializers, the other two keyed on
// `start_with?`/`include?` predicates and correctly not reported), the
// co-occurrence holds only in the initializer that motivated this binding.
//
// The block parameter is spelled `req` per the rack-attack documentation, and
// the conjunct keys on that spelling: a project naming it `request`, or
// writing the literal first (`'/auth/sign_in' == req.path`), keeps the
// weakness and is not reported. Both are recall residuals, not exclusions. A
// remediation that keeps the raw comparison and enumerates the suffixed
// spellings (`req.path == '/auth/sign_in' || req.path == '/auth/sign_in.json'`)
// also keeps the token and reports.
//
// `dependency("rack-attack")` is deliberately absent: Ruby projects declare
// their dependencies in the Gemfile, which is not a parsed manifest, so the
// gate would make this binding inert on every real Rails application. The
// `throttle` conjunct carries the technology instead.

binding throttleRawPathEquality {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and node.context.call in ["throttle"]
    and node.context.expr startsWith "req.path=="
  emit issue code.AuthAttemptLimitReview at node
}
